::MCR_U05_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A fair coin is flipped. What is the theoretical probability of heads?
{
=A. 1/2#Correct. A fair coin has two equally likely outcomes and one is heads.
~B. 1/3#This would mean three equally likely outcomes. A coin has heads and tails.
~C. 2/1#This reverses the probability ratio. Probability cannot be greater than 1 here.
~D. 0/2#This would mean heads cannot happen, but heads is one possible outcome.
}
::MCR_U05_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A spinner is spun 40 times and lands on red 9 times. What is the experimental probability of red?
{
~A. 1/4#This might be theoretical for four equal colors, but the question asks for observed data.
=B. 9/40#Correct. Experimental probability uses observed favorable results over total trials.
~C. 31/40#This is the complement count, not the red count.
~D. 40/9#This reverses the ratio. Use red outcomes over total spins.
}
::MCR_U05_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
In a survey of 50 students, 18 chose online tutoring. What is the relative frequency for online tutoring?
{
~A. 32/50#This is the number who did not choose online tutoring, not the online group.
~B. 18/32#This compares online to not online, but relative frequency should use the total surveyed.
=C. 18/50#Correct. Relative frequency is the count in the category divided by the total.
~D. 50/18#This reverses the ratio. Use part over total.
}
::MCR_U05_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
In a two-way table, what is a joint frequency?
{
~A. A row total#A row total is a marginal total, not a joint frequency.
~B. A column total#A column total is a marginal total, not a joint frequency.
~C. The grand total#The grand total is all data in the table, not one joint cell.
=D. A count inside the body of the table#Correct. A joint frequency counts outcomes that fit both categories.
}
::MCR_U05_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A coin was flipped 20 times and landed heads 13 times. Which statement is true?
{
~A. The theoretical probability of heads is 13/20#This uses observed data, so it is experimental probability.
=B. The experimental probability of heads is 13/20#Correct. Experimental probability is based on the observed 13 heads out of 20 flips.
~C. The experimental probability of heads is 1/2#This is the theoretical probability for a fair coin, not the observed result.
~D. The probability of heads must always equal the observed result#Observed results can change from experiment to experiment. The theoretical probability stays based on the model.
}
